      "case": "leaves/locale/parse-case-script-region-variants",
      "discriminates": "canonicalisation is case-normalising, not case-preserving: `--locale zh-cn` is the spelling users actually pass, and it must reach the same tag as zh-CN. The thirteen lower-cased shipped tags plus JA-jp, Ja-JP, ZH-CN and EN are the rows that witness it, because a port that hands the requested string back unchanged answers every one of them wrongly. Nothing is added anywhere in this trace: language.Parse applies no likely-subtags expansion at the pin, so zh-Hans stays zh-Hans and zh-CN stays zh-CN rather than being maximised to zh-Hans-CN, and a Rust dependency that maximises diverges on both. The already-canonical spellings are recorded for the opposite reason -- zh-Hans-CN, zh-Hant-TW, ja-Latn-JP and sr-Latn-RS pin the script subtag as title case, so a port that upper-cases every non-language subtag by hand answers zh-HANS-CN, and es-419 and en-001 pin a numeric region as passed through untouched",
      "go_callers": [
        "tsc/internal/tsoptions/parsedcommandline.go:491 (keeps the tag and discards ok)",
        "tsc/internal/tsoptions/parsedbuildcommandline.go:42 (keeps the tag and discards ok)",
