      "case": "leaves/locale/parse-aliases-and-legacy-tags",
      "discriminates": "the pinned canonicalisation table rewrites deprecated ISO codes (in->id, iw->he, ji->yi, mo->ro-MD, sh->sr-Latn, tl->fil), grandfathered tags (i-klingon->tlh, art-lojban->jbo, zh-guoyu->cmn, en-GB-oed->en-GB-oxendict, sgn-BE-FR->sfb) and the root alias (root->und), and it drops a redundant primary language in front of an extlang (zh-cmn-Hans-CN->cmn-Hans-CN). The identities are the other half of the evidence and discriminate just as sharply: cmn is *not* folded into zh, no is not normalised to nb, and und and und-Latn come back exactly as written. This is the row that decides whether a Rust dependency has to ship the CLDR alias data at all, and which alias rules it must not apply",
