      "case": "leaves/locale/parse-malformed-and-unsupported",
      "discriminates": "a syntax error and a subtag that is well-formed but unknown are two different failures that both leave ok=false, and either can still hand back a partially parsed tag, which `p.locale, _ = locale.Parse(...)` then keeps. A port that returns Option<Locale> and substitutes the default on any error would change what en-, zh-Xxxx and en--US compile under. Unknown is narrower than it looks and the trace pins where the line falls: zz and the script Xxxx are rejected with a language.ValueError naming the subtag, while the region ZZ (en-ZZ) and the reserved-range language qqq are accepted with ok=true. The row also separates 'parses but no table ships' (nl-NL, sv-SE, ar-SA) from 'does not parse', and fixes whether the underscore spelling ja_JP and the -u- extension survive at all",
