      "case": "leaves/locale/string-default-sentinel",
      "discriminates": "Locale.String() short-circuits to \"\" for the Default sentinel, and that sentinel is the zero Tag rather than a failure: the explicitly requested und tag, the root alias that canonicalises to it, and the two inputs here whose parse salvages nothing (\"\" and zz) all print the empty string while their underlying language.Tag prints und. A Rust Display that forwards to the tag would print \"und\" where the compiler prints nothing. ok=false does not imply the empty string -- the sibling case leaves/locale/parse-malformed-and-unsupported observes en- failing with \"en\" and zh-Xxxx failing with \"zh\" -- and neither does an und prefix: und-Latn is a non-zero tag and prints itself",
      "go_callers": [
        "tsc/internal/locale/locale.go:15-20 compares against the package var Default"
      ],
